save(i,dict(title='Explicit dense robust graphs with efficient local self-ordering',formal='Historical construction question, resolved affirmatively: construct an infinite family of simple undirected graphs G_n=([n],E_n) with Ω(n) robust self-ordering and an efficient local procedure that recovers the canonical name of any vertex in an arbitrary relabelled copy. More precisely, the family has an absolute γ>0 such that |E_n△π(E_n)|≥γn·|{v:π(v)≠v}| for every vertex permutation π, together with a uniform randomized adjacency-query algorithm that recovers the canonical name of a requested vertex with probability at least 2/3 using poly(log n) time. Corollary 1.7 of Goldreich’s checked revision supplies this family and additionally computes its own adjacency predicate in deterministic poly(log n) time, meeting the source’s efficient-construction requirement.',definitions='A simple undirected graph has vertex set [n]={1,…,n} and an edge set E_n of unordered pairs of distinct vertices. For a permutation π of [n], π(E_n)={{π(u),π(v)}:{u,v}∈E_n}. The symmetric difference A△B consists of elements belonging to exactly one of A,B, so the robustness inequality counts changed unordered edges. A graph is self-ordered if its only automorphism is the identity; positive robustness implies this. Given a graph H=([n],F) isomorphic to G_n, there is therefore a unique bijection φ:[n]→[n] satisfying {u,v}∈F iff {φ(u),φ(v)}∈E_n. The local algorithm receives n and a vertex v of H in binary, can query whether any pair is adjacent in H, and must output φ(v). Correctness is required for each H and each v separately, with probability over its internal coins. It is promised an exact isomorphic copy, not a graph with adversarial edge corruptions. Queries are adaptive and each answer is one bit; the polylogarithmic running time counts ordinary computation as well as oracle calls. The family’s canonical adjacency algorithm receives (n,u,v) in binary and decides {u,v}∈E_n. Uniform means fixed algorithms across the family, with no n-dependent advice; poly(log n) means C(log₂(n+1))^a for constants C,a independent of n,H,v. The statement concerns the asymptotic infinite family asserted by the construction; finitely many small sizes do not affect its guarantees. The oracle is the adjacency predicate of a dense graph, not an oracle listing a selected neighbor.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='The affirmative resolution must simultaneously supply the robust graph family, its efficient construction and a polylogarithmic-time local self-ordering algorithm under arbitrary relabelling. The cited Corollary 1.7 provides these guarantees. Mere existence of graphs with few-query but computationally unbounded recovery, or a polynomial-time algorithm reading the full graph, would not have answered the source question.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Recover one canonical vertex name from a relabelled graph',text='An asymmetric graph gives every vertex a unique identity determined by the full graph. Local self-ordering asks whether that identity can be recovered from a small number of adjacency queries. Robust self-ordering separately requires every nontrivial relabelling to change many edges.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The extraction mixed two different footnotes',text='The saved excerpt began with a probability-distribution sentence from footnote 44. The actual open question is footnote 45: an efficient construction of dense, linearly robust graphs supporting local self-ordering. The source already distinguished this from information-theoretic existence.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The later construction achieves both properties',text='Goldreich’s Theorem 1.6 preserves linear robustness while producing graphs with polylogarithmic-time local self-ordering. Corollary 1.7 states the resulting explicit family and also gives a polylogarithmic-time canonical adjacency predicate. These are the simultaneous guarantees missing from the original paragraph.',citation='solution'),dict(title='A different universal implication remains separate',text='The same revision lists as Problem 1.5 whether every linearly robust dense graph has a polylogarithmic-query local procedure. Constructing one family with both properties answers this card, without establishing that universal implication.',citation='solution')],why='Provides efficiently accessible canonical vertex identities in dense graphs while retaining strong asymmetry, enabling explicit constructions in graph property testing.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2021',text='Footnote 45 of the source asks for efficient dense robust graphs with local self-ordering.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025-02-07',text='The checked revision states the affirmative construction in Corollary 1.7, with both local recovery and adjacency computation in polylogarithmic time.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2025',text='The work appears in Computational Complexity and Local Algorithms, pp. 237–281.',citation='publication')],status='resolved',status_note='Matched the actual footnote-45 question to Definitions 1.1–1.2, Theorem 1.6 and Corollary 1.7 of the complete 7 February 2025 author revision, including the stated time bounds and dense adjacency model. Checked the version history and publisher contents through 12 September 2026. No independent proof audit or proof-assistant certificate is claimed.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='Links explicit strong graph asymmetry to efficient access to canonical vertex identities, with concrete property-testing applications. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),review_note='Discarded the unrelated fragment from footnote 44, recovered the construction target in footnote 45 and matched all requested guarantees to the later explicit construction. Marked resolved without conflating this existence theorem with the remaining universal robustness-to-locality question.',working_summary=dict(sentences=['A robustly self-ordered graph changes many edges whenever many vertex names are permuted.','Local self-ordering recovers one vertex’s canonical name using only a few adjacency queries.','The source asks for efficiently constructed dense graphs having both properties.','Goldreich’s later Corollary 1.7 supplies both properties with polylogarithmic time bounds.','The original construction question is resolved, while a universal implication for every robust dense graph is a different problem.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','versions','publication'])),notes=['Identified the true target in footnote 45 and removed neighboring extracted material.','Defined robustness, arbitrary relabelling and the dense adjacency-query local model.','Matched simultaneous efficient construction and local-recovery guarantees to Corollary 1.7.','Marked resolved and distinguished the separate universal question.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
